<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Emanuel Swedenborg</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1688">1688</a>–<a href="/years/1772">1772</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> · Also known as Emanuel Swedberg</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Emanuel Swedenborg was a Swedish scientist, engineer, anatomist, Christian theologian, philosopher and mystic. After studying at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uppsala">Uppsala</a> and spending four years in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a>, he was appointed assessor-extraordinary on the Swedish Board of Mines by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-xii-of-sweden">Charles XII</a> and worked for two decades on natural science and engineering. From 1744 he described dreams and visions, and he held that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesus">Jesus Christ</a> had appointed him to write The Heavenly Doctrine to reform <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a>. Over the last 28 years of his life he wrote 18 published theological works, among them Heaven and Hell (1758). He died in 1772, and the New Church, founded in 1787, reveres his writings as revelation.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/emanuel-swedenborg.jpg" alt="Emanuel Swedenborg" width="330" height="396" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Carl Frederik von Breda · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Emanuel_Swedenborg.PNG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
